# gen-sql.aon --- CREATE TABLE statements from model.aon.
#
# Reads the `sql` slice and ignores `go` entirely. Identifiers are
# QUOTED, because `order` is a reserved word in SQL and a generator
# that emits it bare produces a file that does not parse -- the
# reserved-word problem every code generator meets, answered here by
# always quoting rather than by carrying a word list.
#
# THE TRAILING COMMA IS GONE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF G9 PHASE 2.
# Every column line used to end in `,`, including the last, so this
# output was NOT valid SQL and check.sh asserted the refusal: a
# separator BETWEEN N items is a fold, and the language had none.
# `join(bag, sep)` landed as G9 phase 2 and `body` below is that fold.
# check.sh now hands the output to a real SQL parser and requires it to
# PARSE -- the assertion inverted, which is the whole difference
# between this directory and a code generator.
#
# `rows` IS NOT HIDDEN, AND THAT IS STILL DELIBERATE. The obvious
# spelling wraps the staged intermediate in `hide()` so the scaffolding
# stays out of the evaluated value. It does not survive the parity
# probe: inside `hide()` the Go port never resolves the spread
# template's `.n`, so the transform generates in TypeScript and refuses
# in Go with `mapval_no_gen` -- opposite exit codes on the same
# document (use-cases/BUGS.md 63, still open). Left unhidden, both
# ports agree byte for byte, which check.sh asserts.
@"./model.aon"

units: [
  &: {
    head: `CREATE TABLE "` + .sql + `" (`
    rows: [
      &: {
        out: `  "` + .sql + `" `
          + match(.t, "string", `TEXT`, "integer", `BIGINT`)
          + match(.req, true, ` NOT NULL`, false, ``)
      }
    ] & .fields
    body: join(pick(.rows, out), `,\n`)
    tail: `);`
    text: .head + `\n` + .body + `\n` + .tail
  }
] & $.records

# THE WHOLE FILE, one string.
file: join(pick($.units, text), `\n\n`) + `\n`
